00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:02.790 align:start position:0%
 
an american werewolf in london wasn't  just a hit with audiences it also  inspired the king of pop to make one of  his most iconic videos keep watching for  more inside information on this horror  comedy classic  can i have a piece of toast  in an interview with indy wire director  john landis described how he began  working on the screenplay for an  american werewolf in london in 1969 when  he was still a teenager while serving as  a production assistant during the  filming of kelly's heroes in yugoslavia  landis and a co-worker were traveling  down a road when they witnessed a gypsy  funeral the recently deceased man was  wrapped in garlic and buried feet first  in an abnormally deep grave with the  hope that the precautions would prevent  his rising from the dead i kept thinking  what if he did get up the experience  inspired landis to start writing a  horror story and it didn't take him long  to finish the script convinced that an  encounter with the undead was one of the  most terrifying things he could  experience he was intrigued with the  idea of how an 18 year old like himself  would deal with such a horrifying  situation however the idea did not  garner much interest at first so he was  forced to stow it away for more than a  decade over the course of that decade  landis made a name for himself with a  string of well-received comedies that  included the kentucky fried movie animal  house and the blues brothers with his  career established the director was  finally able to make his werewolf film  in 1981.  even though the funeral rights that  inspired landis were driven by a fear of  zombies he wanted to go in another  direction for his film he later  explained that he picked werewolves  because of their international appeal  with stories in just about every culture  landis was also more interested in the  complex nature of the werewolf character  for the featured monster as he later  explained i really took from the lon  chaney wolfman picture because what that  added was this element of tragedy  historically people in france and wales  were burned to death for being  werewolves or witches and the film made  it a curse where the wolf man himself is  a victim all this history and complexity  means werewolves are never too far from  the popular imagination although landis  had to wait years before he could get  american werewolf and london made it  arrived at a real moment for the beasts  the howling wolfen and full moon high  were all released the same year nice  doggie  good boy john landis wanted poor weather  throughout the movie so he scheduled  filming for february and march this was  especially difficult for lead actor  david naughton who had to run around  without clothing outside in the freezing  cold in an interview with the sf site  the actor recalled that's rather  difficult to do because it's cold and  you've got no shoes on and i don't jog  in bare feet in any weather even back in  california while the production was set  up in a small town in wales there was  such bizarre weather that at one point  it snowed sleeted rained and had periods  of clear sunshine all in the same day  landis always wanted the right look even  if it was very uncomfortable for the  actors as notton recalled we were in  england and it's not raining so they  made rain why would you make rain in  england we're out there on the moors  with these rain machines it was so cold  the rain machines were freezing  renowned makeup and special effects  expert rick baker put in meticulous  detail to create the american werewolf  visuals that are still admired today  horror site bloody disgusting later  recounted the many months of prep work  for the transformation scene when the  audience first sees naughton's painful  and frightening metamorphosis into a  werewolf when baker met david naughton  for the first time he knew what he was  going to put the actor through and said  quote i feel sorry for you to get into  his werewolf makeup knotten left for  work at 4 30 am and sat still in a chair  for 10 full hours until he was ready for  filming the process he described as  being similar to a long flight that  never actually landed the actor  reportedly complained so incessantly  that during a panel landis and baker  once told him to stop making it sound  like he survived torture  you're not real  i don't be a pets david co-star griffin  dunn's experience was also unpleasant  but not as bad as naughton's when  talking about baker in the process dunn  later recalled i hated the makeup i  loved baker he made the six hours  bearable the makeup's on and then it  constricts and then it pulls on your  skin and then it has to be loosened up  you have people fussing with you you  want to pass out from the fumes  david naughton may have been vocal about  the torturous process he had to endure  but there's no doubt that he respected  rick baker's impressive techniques as  did the rest of the film community not  only did baker win an oscar for his  revolutionary makeup effects but as  noted by the academy of motion picture  arts and sciences he was the first to  ever earn an award for an entirely new  category that was created in 1981 for  his craft american werewolf in london  continued a partnership that landis and  baker struck up on the director's first  film 1973's schlock when both men were  barely in their 20s even back then  landis was pitching baker on his  revolutionary transformation scene  telling him he wanted the film to quote  show the pain it paid off and remains  one of the most memorable scenes in  horror movie history  [Music]  it may have been cheaper to film the  movie in the uk but john landis ran into  some serious problems including a major  casting issue landis wanted to put  people of color in the film in order to  reflect the cultural diversity of london  in real life but it was hard to find  actors that were members of local unions  and also people of color still the  director didn't take no for an answer  landis spent much of the production  fighting to show the quote multicultural  nature of the title city and kept  fighting until he got the faces he  wanted on screen  while landis was making sure all the  necessary steps were taken so that the  film could be made in the united kingdom  the british government informed him that  he needed four work permits for himself  rick baker david naughton and griffin  dunn things got testy when the director  later learned that despite the  requirement the government didn't want  to give out more than three permits  while naughton landis and baker easily  got the permits the local actors union  initially prevented dunn from working in  the country because it preferred the  role to be played by an american actor  already living in the uk again landis  remained firm threatening to change the  location of the story to france and  modify the screenplay accordingly as a  sign of how serious he was landis even  scouted possible places to shoot in the  rival country instead according to total  film in the end landis hardball tactics  worked and dunn was awarded his permit  france lost the first round but when an  american werewolf in london received a  long delayed sequel in 1997 landis  threats proved inspirational an american  werewolf in paris moved the story's  action across the english channel  the tense climax of american werewolf in  london takes place at piccadilly circus  which is an often chaotic intersection  bustling with traffic in order to film  scenes of the werewolf rampaging through  the crowded area landis needed to prove  to the right people that he could pull  it off without shutting the whole place  down landis already had success working  with the chicago police for the blues  brothers which seems likely to have been  useful when approaching officials in  london after inviting 300 police  officers to attend a free screening of  the blues brothers the director was  allowed to film at the piccadilly circus  the first time that had been allowed in  15 years according to dread central to  help seal the deal landis recreated the  whole area on a stage so the crew could  rehearse and get it perfect before  filming actually began  for the movie soundtrack landis wanted  to highlight the werewolf themes with  songs that had the word moon in the  title he managed to get the rights to  three versions of blue moon van  morrison's moon dance and creedence  clearwater revival's bad moon rising all  songs that fit the theme the first  rendition of blue moon used in the film  is bobby vinton's followed by sam cooks  and then the version performed by the  marcels landis may have succeeded in  securing those tracks but he failed to  secure others he wanted very badly  possibly on the top of that list was  moon shadow by cat stevens who had  recently converted to islam and had some  objections not only was stevens opposed  to having his work associated with  supernatural themes but he didn't want  any of his songs featured in a movie  that was rated r similar to stevens bob  dylan refused to give permission to use  his version of blue moon and elvis  presley's rendition was simply  unavailable to anybody  see you next wednesday is a short adult  movie within the film that the american  werewolf crew actually made  landis originally planned to have his  hero visit a cartoon theater in london's  piccadilly area because he'd seen such  venues in the 1970s by the time the  1980s rolled around and he returned to  london though all the local theaters had  begun showing adult movies lantis  changed the script to reflect the local  landscape and see you next wednesday was  one of the first things shot for the  film  good movie  see you next wednesday is playing at the  piccadilly circus cinema near the end of  an american werewolf in london when the  protagonist is confronted by the undead  specters of the innocents he slaughtered  in his beastly form  john landis wanted an american werewolf  in london to be a true horror film that  terrified audiences but he was careful  to make sure it was also tame enough to  secure an r rating meaning the sex  scenes weren't too gratuitous and he had  to lose a shot showing a piece of toast  falling out of griffin dunn's ripped  open throat there were other gory shots  and scenes that landis did film but  ended up removing before the theatrical  release a longer scene showing the  werewolf brutally killing a group of  three homeless men was also taken out  because there was such a negative  reaction from test audiences landis got  his r rating but he later admitted that  he regretted toning down the horror  an american werewolf in london took some  time to acquire its cult status but it  had some major fans right away including  pop star michael jackson who liked john  landis and rick baker's work so much  that he asked the filmmakers to make the  music video for his song thriller in  1983. jackson reached out to landis  directly and had just one major request  at first he wanted to turn into a  monster himself in the video landis and  baker were quickly on board for the  collaboration with the king of pop along  with fellow american werewolf veterans  producer george folsey jr and landis  wife costume designer deborah landis the  team also included cinematographer  robert painter and composer elmer  bernstein making thriller a mini  american werewolf reunion the pop star  was certainly pleased with the results  he had landis direct another one of his  music videos black or white in 1991.  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite horror movies are coming  soon subscribe to our youtube channel  and hit the bell so you don't miss a  single one  [Music]

